add(2005,2,4.5,'Geometry',['area ratios','cevians','centroid','extremal argument'],r'Points D,E,F lie in the interiors of BC,CA,AB of triangle ABC, and AD,BE,CF meet at G. If triangles AGF,CGE,BGD have equal area, prove G is the centroid.',r'Write x=[BGC], y=[CGA], z=[AGB], all positive. The usual area ratios along the cevians give AF/AB=y/(x+y), CE/CA=x/(x+z), BD/BC=z/(y+z). Hence the three equal small areas are yz/(x+y), xy/(x+z), xz/(y+z). Dividing their reciprocals by the common product xyz shows $x(x+y)=y(y+z)=z(z+x)$. Relabel cyclically so x is a maximum. Then $x(x+y)-y(y+z)=(x^2-y^2)+y(x-z)\ge0$, with equality only when x=y=z. Thus all three large subtriangle areas are equal, so G has equal barycentric coordinates and is the centroid.','The equal small triangles do not immediately identify midpoint cevians. Expressing their areas through the three large subtriangles creates a cyclic system that an extremal comparison resolves.')
resolve_hold(2007,4)
add(2007,4,4.5,'Combinatorics',['Ramsey theory','graph theory','degree bounds'],r'Two airlines divide all direct routes between six cities, with exactly one airline serving each unordered pair. Prove one airline operates a four-city round trip.',r'One airline operates at least eight of the fifteen routes. We prove every graph on six vertices with eight edges contains a four-cycle. Suppose not. Choose a vertex v of maximum degree d. If d≥4, no other vertex can have two neighbours among the neighbours of v, or it would complete a four-cycle with v. Thus the edges inside that neighbour set form a matching. If d=5, there are at most5+2=7 edges. If d=4, the one remaining vertex has at most one edge into that neighbour set, giving at most4+2+1=7. Otherwise d≤3. Eight edges force d=3. Its three neighbours have at most one internal edge, and each of the other two vertices has at most one edge to those neighbours. Including their possible mutual edge gives at most3+1+2+1=7, again a contradiction. A four-cycle is exactly the required round trip.','Reducing to the denser airline is only the first step. A sharp six-vertex extremal bound requires separate maximum-degree cases and common-neighbour restrictions.')
resolve_hold(2007,9)
add(2007,9,4.5,'Number Theory',['factorials','valuations','last nonzero digit'],r'Find the number of trailing zeros of2007!, and its last nonzero decimal digit.',r'The number of factors of five is401+80+16+3=500, while there are more factors of two, so there are500 trailing zeros. Let U=2007!/5⁵⁰⁰. Modulo five, remove multiples of five repeatedly. For each quotient q=2007,401,80,16,3, the product of its nonmultiples of five is $(-1)^{\lfloor q/5\rfloor}(q\bmod5)!$ by Wilson’s theorem. Thus $U\equiv(-1)^{500}2!1!0!1!3!\equiv2\pmod5$. Dividing out the corresponding500 factors of two changes nothing modulo five because2⁵⁰⁰≡1. The remaining integer2007!/10⁵⁰⁰ is even and congruent to2 modulo five, so its last digit is2.','Counting zeros is routine, but the final nonzero digit requires recursively stripping factors of five and then accounting for the removed twos modulo five.')
resolve_hold(2010,2)
add(2010,2,4,'Geometry',['vectors','midpoint','cevians','equal distances'],r'P is the midpoint of BC in triangle ABC. Interior points M on AB and N on AC have D=MN∩AP and AD=DM=2DN. Prove AC=BC.',r'Use D as the origin and write A=u, M=v. The length condition gives |u|=|v| and N=−v/2, since D lies inside the segment MN. Because M,N are interior side points, write B=(1−t)u+tv and C=(1−s)u−sv/2 with t,s>1. The midpoint P lies on line AD, so its v coefficient vanishes: t−s/2=0. Thus s=2t. We get A−C=t(2u+v) and B−C=t(u+2v). Their squared norms differ by3t²(|u|²−|v|²)=0. Hence AC=BC.','The midpoint condition determines the otherwise unknown side ratios. The equal lengths AD and DM then cancel the remaining vector norm difference.')
